Higher is better. These are anonymous track-matching tests, not proven speaker identities.

The surprising part?

Simply connecting all the new intelligence together actually made the original ranking system worse.

The combined result dropped from 25 correct matches to 18.

Think about five detectives trying to solve a mystery.

Two detectives are really good at recognizing voices. Three others are less reliable. If everybody gets an equal vote, the weaker detectives can outvote the stronger ones.

That's a possible explanation for what you're seeing.

It doesn't mean the other measurements are useless. It means giving every measurement an equal say isn't necessarily a good idea.

## 3. But then you tried something smarter

Instead of making every detective's vote equally important, you tested a trained system that learns how to combine the evidence.

You also added something called cohort normalization.

Imagine you're trying to identify which kid is talking.

Instead of asking just:

"Does this sound like Charlie?"

You ask:

"Does this sound more like Charlie than it sounds like the other kids in this classroom?"

That's the basic intuition.

And look what happened in your separate eight-query test:

| Voice model       | Voice alone | With 3 signals + cohort |
| ----------------- | ----------- | ----------------------- |
| WeSpeaker         | 7/8         | 7/8                     |
| SpeechBrain ECAPA | 7/8         | 8/8                     |
| Resemblyzer       | 7/8         | 8/8                     |
| NVIDIA TitaNet    | 7/8         | 8/8                     |

Three of the four models matched all eight test queries to the existing machine references.

These results come from a different test than the 32 comparisons above. They cannot be compared directly, and 8/8 does not establish real-world identity accuracy.

The additional Brier scores also generally improved with cohort normalization. That measures how well the scores agreed with the machine references, including how confidently they did so. Lower is better.

That is your most promising finding. The extra evidence may be useful when you combine it intelligently, rather than just throwing everything into one pile.

## 4. How much of the actual system is working?

Most of the experimental machinery ran successfully.

Working

Voice fingerprints, alternative voice models, grammar measurements, speaking behavior, conversation events, clustering comparisons, score calibration, and trained fusion.

Not fully available

Fine-grained phrase and pause patterns couldn't be learned because word-level timestamps were missing. Two optional model packages were unavailable.

Safely separated

The experiment didn't automatically assign names, change the production identity resolver, or approve anybody's identity.

You also processed hundreds of little audio clips, collected 446 conversation events, and kept records of what worked and what failed.

That's important because experiments are much more useful when you can repeat them and understand where the results came from.

## 5. What doesn't this prove yet?

Imagine your system watches one classroom video and gets really good at telling the students apart.

That's great.

But what happens when those same kids show up next week?

What if Charlie has a cold? What if he starts whispering? What if the microphone changes? What if somebody else talks exactly like him?

Your experiment hasn't answered those questions.

It used one recording from October 1, and its answers were compared with existing machine-generated speaker links, not verified real-person identities.

So we cannot yet say it recognizes people accurately, works across different meetings, or is ready for production.

## 6. What I would do next

I'd run three experiments:

1. Different recording: Can a fingerprint built on Monday recognize the same anonymous speaker on Thursday?
2. Voice changes: Can it still recognize someone who whispers, shouts, laughs, or speaks emotionally?
3. Harder comparisons: Can it separate two people who sound very similar without falsely combining them?

And I'd add human-verified examples so we can measure true accuracy rather than agreement with another computer.

## The big picture

You're moving from a computer that asks:

"Do these two audio clips sound alike?"

Toward a computer that asks:

"Do these two clips contain the same person's distinctive combination of voice, language habits, rhythm, and conversational behavior?"

That's a much more ambitious problem.

Your results show that the individual components are largely functioning, and the smarter combination method looks promising. They also demonstrate that adding more evidence can make results worse when the evidence isn't weighted properly.

The next big breakthrough wouldn't be getting 8 out of 8 on this recording. It would be recognizing the same anonymous person across completely different recordings, without ever needing to know their name.

That would turn your experimental person-print into something potentially much more useful for diarization and speaker continuity.
